## 📊 Key Visualisations

| Chart | Description |
|---|---|
| `bernoulli_binomial.png` | Bernoulli bar chart + Binomial PMF vs observed weekly counts |
| `poisson_distribution.png` | Poisson PMF vs observed daily counts + daily timeline |
| `lognormal_powerlaw.png` | Histogram with Log-Normal and Power Law PDF overlays |
| `qqplot.png` | Q-Q plots for raw and log-transformed amounts |
| `boxcox_transform.png` | Before/after histograms showing Box-Cox effect |
| `zscore_analysis.png` | Z-score distribution with ₹5000 threshold shaded |
| `pdf_cdf.png` | KDE + Log-Normal PDF and ECDF + CDF overlay |
| `best_fit_comparison.png` | All 3 distributions overlaid with KS statistics |

---

## 🧠 Final Conclusions

| Insight | Business Action |
|---|---|
| **44.5% success rate** (Bernoulli) | Investigate root cause of 55.5% failures |
| **λ = 7.1 transactions/day** (Poisson) | Plan server capacity for ~8 transactions/day peak |
| **Log-Normal best fits amounts** | Use Log-Normal for risk modelling and pricing |
| **11.4% exceed ₹5000** (Z-score) | Flag Z > 0.83 transactions for fraud review |
| **Box-Cox λ ≈ −0.18** | Apply transformation before any normality-based test |
| **Log-Normal confirmed by KS Test** | Adopt Log-Normal for VaR, anomaly detection models |

> **🏆 Best Distribution**: Log-Normal distribution provides the best statistical fit for transaction amounts, confirmed by the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, Q-Q plot alignment, and visual inspection.

---

## 🚀 How to Run

### Prerequisites
```bash
pip install numpy pandas matplotlib seaborn scipy statsmodels openpyxl
```

### Steps
1. Clone or download this repository
2. Place `1780737668182_transaction.xlsx` in the same folder as the notebook
3. Launch Jupyter:
   ```bash
   jupyter notebook Statistical_Distribution_Analysis.ipynb
   ```
4. Run all cells top-to-bottom (`Kernel → Restart & Run All`)

